      "model": "gpt-6.2-sol",
      "output": "{\n  \"choice\": \"within\",\n  \"base_and_horizon\": {\n    \"text\": \"As of May 8, 2020, my base case is within guidance for Nintendo’s fiscal year ending March 31, 2021. Nintendo guided to 200,000 million yen in consolidated profit attributable to owners of parent (PDF p. 2). The inclusive band is 200,000 million yen × (1 − 2%) through 200,000 million yen × (1 + 2%).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P1\"]\n  },\n  \"causal_driver\": {\n    \"text\": \"The main driver is whether Nintendo can make up delayed Switch shipments by year-end. Nintendo reported that production and shipping delays were easing, although component procurement remained a risk (PDF p. 3). Management expected full-year supply to meet demand and software to launch as planned (PDF p. 4). My base case is that recovering hardware sales within the fiscal year, alongside scheduled software sales, keeps full-year profit within the band.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P3\", \"P4\", \"P5\"]\n  },\n  \"alternative\": {\n    \"text\": \"Lower: If COVID-19 prolongs component shortages, Nintendo may be unable to make up lost Switch shipments by March. The resulting loss of hardware sales, and potentially related software sales, could push profit below the band (PDF pp. 3–4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P3\", \"P4\", \"P5\"]\n  },\n  \"revision_signal\": {\n    \"text\": \"I would switch to lower if an interim results update cut the full-year Switch shipment forecast because component shortages were persisting and Nintendo no longer expected to make up the shortfall by March. That would overturn the full-year supply recovery assumption (PDF pp. 3–4).\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P3\", \"P4\", \"P5\"]\n  },\n  \"historical_comparison\": {\n    \"text\": \"The guide was 22.7% below consolidated profit for the year ended March 31, 2020 (PDF p. 2). The prior-year amount can only be approximated from the rounded decline in the excerpt: 200,000 million yen ÷ (1 − 22.7%). The separate 210,842 million yen figure is non-consolidated and is not the like-for-like comparator.\",\n    \"evidence_ids\": [\"P1\", \"P2\"]\n  }\n}",
      "provider": {
